Superficial thermal burn from hair styling iron to neck
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Circumscribed geometric erythema, Brisk dermal blanching, Heated cosmetic tool mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-040
Reason for Visit: Acute contact burn to the neck requiring evaluation of airway proximity, burn depth, and sensitive aesthetic wound care.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute burn from a styling iron to the highly vascular and cosmetically sensitive region of the neck. Urgent bedside clinical assessment was mandated to quickly exclude impending airway compromise from deep tissue swelling, assess dermal integrity, and implement immediate specialized hydrogel dressings to minimize permanent scarring before the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I accidentally clamped my hair straightener against my neck; it burned instantly and is throbbing.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of accidental transient contact with a heated cosmetic tool (hair styling iron) 20 minutes ago. This resulted in an immediate, sharply circumscribed localized burn matching the rectangular profile of the styling wand, located on the lateral neck. The patient describes immediate sharp stinging pain transitioning to an intense dull burn. There is a highly localized surrounding flare of histamine-mediated pink skin. Patient denies any difficulty swallowing, breathing, or voice changes.
Functional Status: Fully independent; holding the head slightly tilted away from the burn.
Social/Safety Concerns: Requires caution during daily cosmetic routines.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, in mild localized distress, visibly avoiding head movements that stretch the neck skin.
Physical Exam: Dermatological assessment of the lateral neck reveals a 2x8cm circumscribed geometric erythema with an erythematous base that briskly blanches under pressure, indicating intact dermal microcirculation. There is a small area of focal epidermal blistering. Complete absence of full-thickness skin loss. Airway assessment is completely clear with no stridor, hoarseness, or deep anterior neck swelling. Normal active neck range of motion is maintained, limited only by the stretching of the painful skin.
Environmental Observations: Styling tools safely unplugged.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Applied a cold damp cloth prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial thermal burn to lateral neck - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Full-thickness thermal burn - Ruled out due to: Presence of extreme pain (indicating intact superficial nerves) and brisk capillary refill within the wound bed.

Airway inhalation/thermal injury - Ruled out due to: Purely external mechanical contact mechanism with no inhalation of superheated gases, normal voice, and stable respirations.

Cellulitis - Ruled out due to: Immediate traumatic thermal mechanism and lack of spreading induration or systemic infectious signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Contact burns to the neck require immediate clinical triage to rule out rapidly expanding deep tissue edema that could compress the airway. Bedside physical examination confidently localized the injury strictly to the superficial dermis without any deep structural or respiratory involvement. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear the airway risk, provide immediate sterile barrier protection to this highly mobile and cosmetically sensitive area, and suppress the severe localized pain, effectively treating the patient safely in the home without necessitating an emergency department transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Gently cleansed the area with sterile saline. Applied a specialized thin, conformable hydrocolloid/hydrogel dressing specifically chosen for high-mobility cosmetic areas to prevent heavy scarring. Tetanus prophylaxis updated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Leave the thin hydrocolloid dressing firmly attached to the neck for 3 to 5 days until it begins to peel off naturally. The dressing creates a perfectly moist, undisturbed healing environment that rapidly regenerates the skin cells, drastically reducing the risk of a permanent, dark geometric scar on the neck.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Thin hydrocolloid patches.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 3-5 days for dressing removal and assessment of cosmetic healing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if you suddenly develop a hoarse voice, any difficulty swallowing your saliva, shortness of breath, or if the swelling rapidly expands across the front of your throat.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Burns on the neck heal well but are highly prone to post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation (darkening) if exposed to sunlight too early.
Clinical Course: The sharp pain will rapidly settle beneath the dressing. The wound will heal over 7 to 10 days. The new skin will be pink and sensitive for several weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the burn site becomes intensely red, hot, or begins discharging thick, smelly fluid.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The specialized dressing acts like a second skin, moving with your neck to prevent the burn from tearing open while keeping bacteria out and the moisture in.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing on in the shower. Do not apply makeup or creams over the dressing. Once the dressing is removed, you must wear SPF 50+ sunscreen on that spot every day for 6 months to prevent a dark scar.

